menu "BYOK Mod"

    config BYOK_STOCK_GPIO_PARITY
        bool "Configure GPIO 35/39/47 OUTPUT-LOW at boot, matching stock exactly"
        default y
        help
            The stock firmware configures GPIOs 17, 35, 39, 42 and 47 as
            OUTPUT and drives all five LOW in one contiguous block, as the
            very first GPIO action of SYS_APP init -- well before any I2C
            bus, the display, or the boot-mode fork (docs/pinout.md
            Sec.5, evidence offsets there). This firmware already
            reproduces GPIO 42 (the power latch, init_power_hold_gpio())
            with the same polarity and boot-time placement; GPIO 17 is
            reserved for a future PICO-reset implementation and is not yet
            driven by anything. GPIOs 35, 39 and 47 were, before this
            option existed, never configured or driven at all -- left in
            their ESP32-S3 reset state (input, floating) instead of stock's
            OUTPUT-LOW.

            35 and 47 are STRONGLY INDICATED S3->PICO handshake lines
            (request/ready-out and transfer-strobe respectively,
            hw_config.h Sec.5); 39 has NO confirmed function anywhere in
            either vendor firmware image (hw_config.h Sec.13
            BYOK_GPIO39_FUNCTION) but is the only one of the three with no
            OTHER candidate role either, which is why
            docs/recovery.md §6 and the 2026-09-03
            blank-display incident (docs/troubleshooting.md) flag it as a
            POSSIBLE
            (not confirmed) panel-supply/level-shifter enable line.

            ON (the default, byok-mod 0.1.6+): configures GPIOs 35, 39 and
            47 as OUTPUT and drives each LOW, in stock's own relative order
            (35, then 47, then 39 -- see docs/pinout.md §5) and
            with stock's own timing (a single gpio_config() call covering
            all three, then three gpio_set_level() calls back-to-back with
            no delay between them, matching the disassembly exactly), as
            early in app_main() as GPIO 42's own latch -- immediately after
            it, before check_boot_button()/byok_power_init()/the SD
            updater/byok_display_init(). Never touches any pin outside this
            three-pin set. Each pin's configuration is logged individually.

            This is a stock-parity change, not a claimed display fix: by
            construction it can never do anything the stock firmware does
            not already do on every boot of this exact board. It is enabled
            by default this release specifically to test the panel-enable
            hypothesis above, alongside the display driver's own ACK probe
            (components/byok_display's byok_display_init()) -- see
            docs/display.md for the full reasoning chain.

            OFF: exact 0.1.5 behaviour -- GPIOs 35/39/47 left unconfigured
            (input, floating), matching every byok-mod release through
            0.1.5.

    config BYOK_ALLOW_OTADATA_WRITE
        bool "Allow BOOT_ORIGINAL to actually write otadata (esp_ota_set_boot_partition)"
        default n
        help
            BOOT_ORIGINAL (docs/protocol.md Sec.6.4, type 0x26) and the
            boot-time EXECUTE-held-3s path (D-011) both call
            esp_ota_set_boot_partition() on the stock app partition, which
            writes the `otadata` partition -- one of the flash regions
            SAFETY.md's hard rules single out as never-erase, never-write
            until "a verified backup AND a tested recovery path both exist"
            (see docs/recovery.md).

            With this OFF (the default), both paths still run their full
            lookup/validation logic but stop just short of the actual OTA
            write: BOOT_ORIGINAL replies NACK/E_NOT_PERMITTED and the
            boot-time button hold logs a warning and does nothing. HELLO_ACK
            and INFO's capability bit 8 (BOOT_ORIGINAL available) also read
            as 0 while this is off, so a connected host's UI does not offer
            a control that would always fail.

            Turn this ON only after the project's own recovery-path gate
            (docs/recovery.md, SAFETY.md §1 / D-003) is actually satisfied
            for this specific device -- not as a build-time convenience.

    config BYOK_USB_CDC_MIN_UPTIME_MS
        int "Minimum uptime (ms) before claiming the USB-OTG PHY for CDC-ACM"
        default 5000
        help
            With CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=n (this device has no
            automatic rollback -- docs/bootloader-analysis.md), esptool over
            USB-Serial-JTAG is the ONLY recovery from a crash-looping image,
            and that recovery window is only open until this app claims the
            native USB-OTG PHY for TinyUSB CDC-ACM (a different USB
            peripheral -- see byok_usb_cdc.h "Two USB personalities"). This
            value is app_main's own minimum boot-to-CDC-init delay, enforced
            with esp_timer_get_time() immediately before
            byok_usb_cdc_init(), independent of how long the boot self-test
            animation happens to run. README.md documents the observed ~4 s
            USJ enumeration window; the default here is set comfortably
            above that.

endmenu
